{:edb/db-before-t 2881 :edb/db-after-t 2882 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536194 50 #inst "2026-10-09T19:21:37.238Z" 13194139536194 true] [17592186124350 1145 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/191451016703b3287c7e58fdb59e22effb919c97dcad7a9e3bec09efd115795a.png) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/3b13b13c14eb335adb58b3d1ff3132b4e189065033481aaf371a9376188f2443.png) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044323/assets/a906a8520f0b2f676bcf97411232fa3303f87aec9dcae9abe19dca00ab6da1a7.png)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536194 false] [17592186124350 1145 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/assets/191451016703b3287c7e58fdb59e22effb919c97dcad7a9e3bec09efd115795a.png) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/assets/3b13b13c14eb335adb58b3d1ff3132b4e189065033481aaf371a9376188f2443.png) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084109/assets/a906a8520f0b2f676bcf97411232fa3303f87aec9dcae9abe19dca00ab6da1a7.png)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536194 true] [17592186124402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388248 13194139536194 true] [17592186124402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388255 13194139536194 true] [17592186124402 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388269 13194139536194 true] [17592186124510 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388276 13194139536194 true] [17592186124510 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388283 13194139536194 true] [17592186124549 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388262 13194139536194 true] [17592186125158 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536194 true] [17592186125158 1100 "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0, ∞)$ to $R$ of the form $f(x) = a\\log_{2} (x)$, where $a ∈ R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n2. For any $α, β ∈ R$ and $f(x) ∈ L$, we have $(α + β)f(x) = αf(x) + βf(x)$.\n3. For any $α ∈ R$ and any $f(x), g(x) ∈ L$, we have $α(f(x) + g(x)) = αf(x) + αg(x)$." 13194139536194 false] [17592186125158 1100 "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0,\\infty )$ to $R$ of the form $f(x)=a{log}_{2}⁡(x),$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n- For any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$ and $f(x)\\in L,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $\\alpha \\in R$ and any $f(x),g(x)\\in L,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" 13194139536194 true] [17592186125158 1145 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388248 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388249 13194139536194 true] [17592186388248 1089 "q-106700" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388248 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536194 true] [17592186388248 1100 "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388248 1113 #uuid "32414fd0-b715-59b8-9662-c3f08db22298" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388248 1145 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in M.$ Then $-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388250 13194139536194 true] [17592186388249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388251 13194139536194 true] [17592186388249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388252 13194139536194 true] [17592186388249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388253 13194139536194 true] [17592186388249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388254 13194139536194 true] [17592186388249 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536194 true] [17592186388249 1041 "selection" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388249 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388251 13194139536194 true] [17592186388249 1110 #uuid "526fbdb6-202c-5b1d-a24d-d458ae47d76c" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388250 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388250 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388250 1111 #uuid "a3cb7deb-769c-5697-9ab8-25c0643b3f53" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388251 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388251 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388251 1111 #uuid "2b73b973-8729-56b1-bd66-c2f9040091a7" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388252 1034 "I and III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388252 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388252 1111 #uuid "b4cf7e2d-3418-5372-ad94-80fdd5ae01a5" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388253 1034 "III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388253 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388253 1111 #uuid "e785aa7c-f7b4-59f0-8fe7-6933c726aea3" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388254 1034 "II only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388254 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388254 1111 #uuid "87f0b589-68fb-5896-8c78-bba15f07790e" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388255 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388256 13194139536194 true] [17592186388255 1089 "q-106710" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388255 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536194 true] [17592186388255 1100 "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative." 13194139536194 true] [17592186388255 1113 #uuid "fd9de429-1f02-596c-8b1c-229012ad598c" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388255 1145 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388257 13194139536194 true] [17592186388256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388258 13194139536194 true] [17592186388256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388259 13194139536194 true] [17592186388256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388260 13194139536194 true] [17592186388256 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388261 13194139536194 true] [17592186388256 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536194 true] [17592186388256 1041 "selection" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388256 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388258 13194139536194 true] [17592186388256 1110 #uuid "22d6cf17-ec29-5184-908b-67e882d88b9b" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388257 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388257 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388257 1111 #uuid "89561ae7-b58b-5494-97a4-ac6caf35ce31" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388258 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388258 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388258 1111 #uuid "bf98801f-8cd1-5f73-afef-61ffbe92ef36" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388259 1034 "I and III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388259 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388259 1111 #uuid "07140bfc-36f9-52a2-a322-e3ac93bc42d0" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388260 1034 "III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388260 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388260 1111 #uuid "1cedddc5-0428-5f11-8c3f-285ce9624563" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388261 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388261 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388261 1111 #uuid "93388bb4-a19d-5497-9a09-f60a2172c78f" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388262 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388263 13194139536194 true] [17592186388262 1089 "q-162379" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388262 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536194 true] [17592186388262 1100 "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p(x)\\in N,$ where $p(x)={x}^{3}-1.$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n- The set is closed under addition." 13194139536194 true] [17592186388262 1113 #uuid "e779aef6-5e8f-5f83-b9cd-2d7a48b1b6c3" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388262 1145 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have, $p(x)={x}^{3}-1\\notin N$ because\n\n$$\np(0)=(0)^{3}-1=-1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x),g(x)\\in N,$ where $f(0)=0$ and $g(0)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nf(0)+g(0)=0+0=0.\n$$\n\nHence $f(x)+g(x)\\in N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388263 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388264 13194139536194 true] [17592186388263 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388265 13194139536194 true] [17592186388263 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388266 13194139536194 true] [17592186388263 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388267 13194139536194 true] [17592186388263 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388268 13194139536194 true] [17592186388263 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536194 true] [17592186388263 1041 "selection" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388263 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388264 13194139536194 true] [17592186388263 1110 #uuid "5adb99a5-c1f3-537c-8592-8f3698140873" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388264 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388264 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388264 1111 #uuid "a7ea0903-d431-594f-8c46-151fd8f520d4" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388265 1034 "I only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388265 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388265 1111 #uuid "977f6cca-e778-56e0-aece-ed0ddb30f051" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388266 1034 "III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388266 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388266 1111 #uuid "19125caf-a058-576b-9f3a-f954d21b7fd1" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388267 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388267 1111 #uuid "dcd17b85-3e53-53d1-8710-27afb2721fe4" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388268 1034 "II only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388268 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388268 1111 #uuid "d0826c64-a507-5ba4-99ab-04474c0955e3" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388269 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388270 13194139536194 true] [17592186388269 1089 "q-162620" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388269 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536194 true] [17592186388269 1100 "Let $G$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero matrix $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $G.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in G,$ we have $A-O=A.$\n- For any $A,B\\in G,$ we have $A-B=B-A.$" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388269 1113 #uuid "f0ad24e1-b135-5213-ba6a-3767c846e774" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388269 1145 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,$O=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]$ with $a=b=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, as for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\nA-O=A+(-O)=A+O=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, let's consider the matrices $O=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then $O$ and ${I}_{2}$ belong to $G,$ and we\nhave \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}-O={I}_{2}\n$$\n\n \nbut \n\n$$\nO-{I}_{2}=-{I}_{2}\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388270 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388271 13194139536194 true] [17592186388270 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388272 13194139536194 true] [17592186388270 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388273 13194139536194 true] [17592186388270 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388274 13194139536194 true] [17592186388270 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388275 13194139536194 true] [17592186388270 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536194 true] [17592186388270 1041 "selection" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388270 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388274 13194139536194 true] [17592186388270 1110 #uuid "6d10bce0-21bd-5047-91f5-ac58688f456e" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388271 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388271 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388271 1111 #uuid "b0f06412-9f71-5a32-a3e8-ceaf2d01270b" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388272 1034 "I only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388272 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388272 1111 #uuid "9a3d99cf-68e5-5ac4-9700-da61ab8446a0" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388273 1034 "III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388273 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388273 1111 #uuid "a9845488-6106-50e3-9a49-2dcddf2b17e8" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388274 1034 "I and II only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388274 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388274 1111 #uuid "64c9ca87-f925-59e3-ba64-032305a73b88" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388275 1034 "II only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388275 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388275 1111 #uuid "3262dc00-40ac-5a79-8549-c4331b07b3b0" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388276 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388277 13194139536194 true] [17592186388276 1089 "q-162665" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388276 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536194 true] [17592186388276 1100 "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\in S$\n- For any $p(x)\\in S,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot p(x)\\in S$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388276 1113 #uuid "a0988a68-ce20-56f3-895e-03e24d1dc996" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388276 1145 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $deg(q)$ the degree of a polynomial $q.$ Then, for any $p(x)\\in S$ we have that $(-1)\\cdot p(x)$ is also a polynomial, and that\n\n$$\ndeg((-1)\\cdot p)=deg(-1)+deg(p)=0+deg(p)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)p(x)\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the values $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388277 1000 #edb/ref 