.$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$\n- For any $p(x)\\in L,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).$" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388283 1113 #uuid "07c5aec0-d0c8-57f8-807e-d869fcad4620" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388283 1145 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=x+1$ and $q(x)=x-1$ from $L.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p(x)+q(x)) & =2(x+1+x-1) \\\\  & =2(2x) \\\\  & =4x \\\\ 2p(x)-2q(x) & =2(x+1)-2(x-1) \\\\  & =2x+2-2x+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388285 13194139536194 true] [17592186388284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388286 13194139536194 true] [17592186388284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388287 13194139536194 true] [17592186388284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388288 13194139536194 true] [17592186388284 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388289 13194139536194 true] [17592186388284 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536194 true] [17592186388284 1041 "selection" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388284 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388286 13194139536194 true] [17592186388284 1110 #uuid "ef3b1d1b-c70b-5761-a037-2adb230ae17c" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388285 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388285 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536194 true] [17592186388285 1111 #uuid "82293cf7-076c-5d8a-9931-a4bc23990b1f" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388286 1034 "II and III only" 13194139536194 true] [17592186388286 1103 #e